dxgi: Report the video memory budget as the adapter's dedicated video memory.

DXMT_VIDEO_MEMORY_BUDGET_MB (patch 0003) set the budget QueryVideoMemoryInfo reports and
nothing else. A game that never asks for the budget, as most Direct3D 11 games do not, plans
its video memory by DXGI_ADAPTER_DESC's DedicatedVideoMemory, which stayed at half of the GPU's
working set: Call of Duty: WWII, told a budget of 5000 MB, still grew the Mac's graphics memory
to 12 GB, and when another app came to the front macOS went to memory pressure "warning" and the
game fell from 120 to 70 frames a second (2026-11-25). The adapter now reports the budget,
whatever set it, as its dedicated video memory. Without the variable nothing changes: the two
were the same number already.

LOCAL TO THIS PORT, NOT FOR DXMT. Applied by macos/build-dxmt.sh to
github.com/athei/dxmt at 01e59e9bde030f105937f249afaac92ada01b4d7, after 0001 to 0010.
Written with an AI assistant (Claude). DXMT does not accept contributions made or
co-authored by AI (its CONTRIBUTING.md), so this patch must not be sent to it as a pull
request.
---
diff --git a/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp b/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp
--- a/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp
+++ b/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp
@@ -178,10 +178,9 @@
 
     pDesc->SubSysId = 0;
     pDesc->Revision = 0;
-    if (device_.hasUnifiedMemory())
-      pDesc->DedicatedVideoMemory = device_.recommendedMaxWorkingSetSize() / 2; // FIXME: use a more appropriate value
-    else
-      pDesc->DedicatedVideoMemory = device_.recommendedMaxWorkingSetSize();
+    // What a game that does not ask for the budget plans its video memory by (Direct3D 11 games
+    // read this once, as they start): the budget, so that DXMT_VIDEO_MEMORY_BUDGET_MB reaches them too
+    pDesc->DedicatedVideoMemory = videoMemoryBudget();
     pDesc->DedicatedSystemMemory = 0;
     pDesc->SharedSystemMemory = 0;
     pDesc->AdapterLuid = luid_;
